CARDS = [
    {"front":"Four elements of a valid insurance contract","back":"Competent parties, legal purpose, offer and acceptance (agreement), and consideration. Memory cue: C-L-O-C."},
    {"front":"Legal purpose","back":"An enforceable contract must serve a lawful purpose and align with public policy."},
    {"front":"Offer and acceptance","back":"In the course summary, the application plus premium is treated as the offer; acceptance is shown by the insurer's approval/issuance or delivery under the applicable process."},
    {"front":"Consideration — applicant","back":"The applicant's consideration includes premium payments and truthful/material statements made in the application."},
    {"front":"Consideration — insurer","back":"The insurer's consideration is its promise to perform according to the contract, including paying covered claims/benefits when policy conditions are met."},
    {"front":"Aleatory contract","back":"Performance can involve an unequal exchange because benefits depend on an uncertain future event."},
    {"front":"Contract of adhesion","back":"Prepared by the insurer on a take-it-or-leave-it basis; ambiguities are generally construed against the drafter and in favor of the insured."},
    {"front":"Unilateral contract","back":"Only the insurer makes the legally enforceable promise to pay covered benefits. The policyowner does not promise to keep paying premiums, but nonpayment can cause lapse or termination."},
    {"front":"Personal contract","back":"Insurance commonly insures the interest/person of a particular insured or policyowner rather than freely attaching to property itself; transferability depends on the line and policy."},
    {"front":"Conditional contract","back":"The insurer's obligation depends on required policy conditions being satisfied, such as premium payment, notice, or proof of loss."},
    {"front":"Valued contract","back":"Pays a stated or predetermined amount when the covered event occurs. Life insurance is the classic example because the death benefit is fixed by the contract rather than calculated as an actual economic loss at death."},
    {"front":"Indemnity contract","back":"Pays based on the covered actual loss, subject to policy terms, with the goal of restoring the insured to approximately the pre-loss financial position rather than creating a gain."},
    {"front":"Insurable interest","back":"A legitimate financial or economic interest in the subject of insurance such that the person would suffer loss if the insured person or property were damaged or lost."},
    {"front":"Life/health insurable interest timing","back":"For the course exam rule, insurable interest must exist when the life/health policy is applied for or issued; it generally does not have to continue through the insured's death."},
    {"front":"Property/casualty insurable interest timing","back":"For the course exam rule, insurable interest must exist at policy inception/application and at the time of loss; a claimant must have a financial interest in the property when the loss occurs."},
    {"front":"STOLI","back":"Stranger-originated life insurance arrangements are prohibited/illegal because they are designed to create life insurance without a legitimate insurable-interest relationship."},
    {"front":"Express authority","back":"Authority specifically written or directly granted in the agent's contract or appointment."},
    {"front":"Implied authority","back":"Unwritten authority reasonably necessary to carry out the agent's express authority and ordinary duties."},
    {"front":"Apparent authority","back":"Authority a reasonable third party believes the agent has because of the insurer/principal's actions, words, branding, tools, or other circumstances."},
    {"front":"Agent vs. broker","back":"Agent represents the insurer and may bind coverage when authorized. Broker represents the buyer/insured and generally cannot bind the insurer without an authorized company representative."},
    {"front":"Solicitor","back":"A solicitor seeks applicants or prospects under limited authority and cannot bind coverage."},
    {"front":"Void contract","back":"Never legally valid or enforceable because an essential legal element is missing or the agreement is unlawful. Neither party can enforce it."},
    {"front":"Voidable contract","back":"A valid contract that one party may elect to terminate or rescind because of a legally recognized reason; rights generally exist until it is voided."},
    {"front":"Waiver","back":"Voluntary and intentional surrender of a known right. Example pattern: an insurer intentionally gives up enforcement of a policy right."},
    {"front":"Estoppel","back":"A legal bar preventing a party from asserting a right when another reasonably relied on that party's representation or conduct and suffered harm."},
    {"front":"Estoppel recognition pattern","back":"Look for representation, reasonable reliance/action, authority/connection to the insurer, and resulting financial harm."},
    {"front":"Warranty","back":"A statement guaranteed to be true and treated as part of the contract; warranties are held to a stricter truth standard than representations."},
    {"front":"Representation","back":"A statement made by an applicant and believed to be true to the best of the applicant's knowledge; a false representation generally must be material to affect the policy."},
    {"front":"Concealment","back":"Failure to disclose a known material fact. The insurer generally must establish materiality and the legal basis for rescission or other remedy."},
    {"front":"Subrogation","back":"After paying a covered claim, the insurer may step into the insured's shoes to recover from the responsible third party, subject to applicable law and policy terms."},
    {"front":"Final-review scenario priorities","back":"Expect short scenarios testing agent authority, estoppel elements, void vs. voidable, insurable-interest timing, and precise legal terminology."},
]

QUESTIONS = [
    {"q":"Which group lists the four basic elements of a valid insurance contract?","choices":["Competent parties, legal purpose, offer/acceptance, consideration","Premium, peril, hazard, subrogation","Agent, broker, solicitor, adjuster","Aleatory, adhesion, unilateral, personal"],"answer":0,"why":"The core elements are competent parties, legal purpose, agreement (offer/acceptance), and consideration."},
    {"q":"A life policy promises a fixed $500,000 death benefit rather than calculating the economic value of the life at death. This is best described as:","choices":["A valued contract","An indemnity contract","A void contract","A subrogation contract"],"answer":0,"why":"Life insurance is a classic valued contract because the amount is predetermined by the policy."},
    {"q":"A property policy reimburses the insured for the amount of a covered loss, subject to policy limits and deductibles. The policy is primarily based on:","choices":["Indemnity","Aleatory exchange only","Estoppel","Warranty"],"answer":0,"why":"Indemnity focuses on compensating actual covered loss and restoring the pre-loss financial position."},
    {"q":"For the course exam rule, when must insurable interest exist in a life insurance policy?","choices":["At application/policy inception","Only at death","Continuously for the life of the policy","Only when a claim is filed"],"answer":0,"why":"Life insurance requires insurable interest when the policy is procured; it generally need not continue until death."},
    {"q":"For property insurance, which timing statement is most accurate for the course exam rule?","choices":["Insurable interest must exist at inception and at the time of loss","Only when the application is signed","Only when the premium is refunded","It is never required"],"answer":0,"why":"A property claimant must have a financial interest in the property when the covered loss occurs."},
    {"q":"An agent's written contract specifically authorizes collecting premiums. What type of authority is this?","choices":["Express authority","Implied authority","Apparent authority","No authority"],"answer":0,"why":"Authority directly written or granted is express authority."},
    {"q":"An agent orders ordinary business cards needed to perform assigned duties, even though the contract does not mention business cards. This illustrates:","choices":["Implied authority","Express authority","Void authority","Subrogation"],"answer":0,"why":"Implied authority covers acts reasonably necessary to carry out express duties."},
    {"q":"A customer reasonably believes a producer can act for an insurer because the insurer provided an official company email address and branded sales tools. This most closely illustrates:","choices":["Apparent authority","Consideration","Concealment","Indemnity"],"answer":0,"why":"Apparent authority arises from the principal's conduct creating a reasonable appearance of authority."},
    {"q":"Which producer generally represents the insurance buyer rather than the insurer?","choices":["Broker","Agent","Insurer's principal","Underwriter only"],"answer":0,"why":"A broker generally represents the insured/buyer and places coverage with insurers."},
    {"q":"Which statement best distinguishes a void contract from a voidable contract?","choices":["Void was never legally enforceable; voidable is valid until a party entitled to reject it does so","Void is always valid; voidable never existed","They are identical","Voidable means no party has rights from inception"],"answer":0,"why":"A void agreement lacks legal validity from the outset; a voidable agreement exists but may be set aside."},
    {"q":"An insurer intentionally gives up enforcement of a known policy right. This is:","choices":["Waiver","Subrogation","Concealment","Indemnity"],"answer":0,"why":"Waiver is the voluntary surrender of a known right."},
    {"q":"A client reasonably relies on an authorized agent's representation, acts on it, and suffers financial harm. Which principle may prevent the insurer from contradicting that representation?","choices":["Estoppel","Subrogation","Aleatory contract","Legal purpose"],"answer":0,"why":"Estoppel protects against unfairly denying a representation when reliance and harm are present under the applicable legal conditions."},
    {"q":"Which statement is held to a stricter truth standard and becomes part of the contract?","choices":["Warranty","Representation","Estimate","Opinion"],"answer":0,"why":"A warranty is guaranteed true and is treated as part of the contract."},
    {"q":"An applicant fails to disclose a known material fact relevant to underwriting. This is most closely associated with:","choices":["Concealment","Subrogation","Indemnity","Apparent authority"],"answer":0,"why":"Concealment is the failure to disclose a known material fact."},
    {"q":"After paying a covered auto claim, the insurer seeks recovery from the at-fault driver's insurer. This is:","choices":["Subrogation","Waiver","Adhesion","Consideration"],"answer":0,"why":"Subrogation allows the paying insurer to pursue the responsible third party for recovery."},
]

SCENARIO_DRILLS = [
    {"scenario":"A policy is drafted entirely by the insurer. The applicant cannot negotiate the language and must accept or reject it as written.","answer":"Contract of adhesion","why":"The insurer drafts the terms; ambiguity is generally construed against the drafter."},
    {"scenario":"A producer performs a routine act necessary to complete an explicitly authorized sale, even though the contract never lists that exact act.","answer":"Implied authority","why":"The act is reasonably necessary to carry out express authority."},
    {"scenario":"An insurer equips a producer with official branding and systems, causing a customer reasonably to believe the producer has authority.","answer":"Apparent authority","why":"The principal's conduct created the appearance of authority."},
    {"scenario":"A life policy was validly issued while a legitimate insurable-interest relationship existed. Years later the relationship ends, but the policy remains in force.","answer":"Life insurable interest generally need not continue after policy inception","why":"The key timing point is insurable interest when the life policy is procured."},
    {"scenario":"A person sells a house and later tries to collect for damage that occurs after ownership has fully transferred.","answer":"No property insurable interest at time of loss","why":"Property coverage requires a financial interest in the property when the loss occurs."},
    {"scenario":"An insurer knowingly accepts conduct inconsistent with a policy right and intentionally gives up enforcing that right.","answer":"Waiver","why":"The insurer voluntarily surrendered a known right."},
    {"scenario":"A customer relies on an authorized agent's statement, changes behavior because of it, then suffers a financial loss when the insurer tries to deny the representation.","answer":"Potential estoppel","why":"Representation, reliance/action, authority, and harm are the key recognition pattern."},
    {"scenario":"An application contains a false statement that would have changed the insurer's underwriting decision.","answer":"Material misrepresentation / representation issue","why":"Applicant statements are representations; a materially false statement can make the contract voidable or support rescission under applicable law."},
    {"scenario":"An applicant knows a serious fact relevant to underwriting but fails to disclose it.","answer":"Concealment","why":"Concealment is failure to disclose a known material fact."},
    {"scenario":"The insurer pays its insured and then pursues the party responsible for causing the loss.","answer":"Subrogation","why":"The insurer steps into the insured's shoes to seek recovery."},
]

